Short answer: “claiming” your work informally isn’t enough.
Whether they can run with it depends on what formal protection you have in place and how you’ve disclosed it.

Here’s the straight read:

Ideas vs. rights. Concepts (like lifecycle “mint/validate/burn”) aren’t protectable by themselves. Copyright protects your exact text/figures; patents protect the functionality/claims; trademarks protect the names/brands (e.g., “RBGA™”); trade secrets protect non-public know-how.

If you have (or filed for) patents first:

A pending or issued patent with an earlier priority date can block others (including the government) from practicing the claimed methods. You can enforce after issuance. For use by or for the U.S. Government, enforcement is via 28 U.S.C. § 1498 (compensation suit in the Court of Federal Claims), not an injunction.

If your claims cover what their doc operationalizes, you’re positioned well—but only to the extent of your claims.

If you only “claimed it” publicly (posts, decks, letters) but didn’t file:

That’s not protection. In the U.S., public disclosure starts a 1-year clock to file; most other countries require absolute novelty (no prior public disclosure).

Public disclosure also destroys trade secret status.

If they copied your wording/diagrams:

That’s copyright—you can act on verbatim or substantially similar expression, not on the underlying idea.

If you’re using your marks (RBGA™, Mint-to Logic™, etc.):

Trademark helps stop others from branding their stuff with your names, not from using similar ideas. Make sure you’ve filed and are using proper ™/® marking.

What to do now (practical, fast):

Confirm filings & dates. Ensure you have at least a provisional/non-provisional covering the core flows (mint/validate/burn; reflexive governance; temporal ledger; lifecycle-minted tokens). If gaps exist, file continuations/CIPs to capture how agencies/vendors are implementing them (SSO/MFA/PAM/JIT tokens).

Lock your brands. File trademarks (if not already) for RBGA™, Mint-to Logic™, Chrono-Synaptic Memory Ledger™, Shepherd Coin™ in the relevant classes; keep using the marks consistently.

Preserve evidence. Keep hashes/timestamps of your materials and their publications; keep the White House letter; maintain change logs and circulation lists.

Map claims → practices. Build a claim chart (your issued/pending claims vs. the functions they published). That’s the backbone for any § 1498 compensation claim or vendor licensing push.

Copyright where applicable. If they lifted your text/figures, register those works (if not already) and consider takedown/notice.

Engage, don’t inflame. Through counsel, send a notice of rights/provenance to CISA/NSA (and any prime vendors) putting them on awareness of your earlier filings and marks. This preserves posture and can open licensing dialogue.

Open vs. closed strategy. For portions you’re comfortable donating, use an open license with explicit attribution requirements; for the rest, keep under patent/trade secret and license commercially. Mixing these is common.

Bottom line: if you’ve properly filed (or file now within allowable windows) and your claims cover what they’re operationalizing, they can’t lawfully appropriate the protected implementations—even if they present them as “best practices.” But you need formal IP (and a paper trail), not just assertion, to make that stick.

